[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Java Collections Hierarchy and Map Internals Technical Tutorial"},{"order":2,"type":"subheading","text":"1. Introduction to the Collections Framework"},{"order":3,"type":"paragraph","text":"The Java Collections Framework (JCF) provides a unified architecture for representing and manipulating collections of objects. At its core, the framework separates the interface (what collections can do) from the implementation (how they do it)."},{"order":4,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Collection Interface: The root of the hierarchy, representing a group of objects known as elements."},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Map Interface: Represents a mapping between keys and values, standing slightly apart from the Collection interface hierarchy."},{"order":6,"type":"subheading","text":"2. List Interface and Common Implementations"},{"order":7,"type":"paragraph","text":"The List interface represents an ordered collection (sometimes called a sequence). Lists allow duplicate elements and provide precise control over where elements are inserted and accessed via integer indices."},{"order":8,"type":"bullet_point","text":"ArrayList: Implemented using a dynamic array. Provides O(1) time complexity for random access, but O(N) for insertions and deletions in the middle."},{"order":9,"type":"bullet_point","text":"LinkedList: Implemented as a doubly-linked list. Provides O(1) performance for insertions and deletions when the node is already located, but O(N) for random access."},{"order":10,"type":"code","text":"List<String> names = new ArrayList<>();
names.add("Alice");
names.add("Bob");
String first = names.get(0);"},{"order":11,"type":"subheading","text":"3. Set Interface and Uniqueness"},{"order":12,"type":"paragraph","text":"A Set is a collection that contains no duplicate elements. It models the mathematical set abstraction and relies heavily on the equals() and hashCode() contracts to ensure uniqueness."},{"order":13,"type":"bullet_point","text":"HashSet: Backed by a HashMap, offering constant time O(1) performance for basic operations (add, remove, contains), assuming the hash function disperses elements properly."},{"order":14,"type":"bullet_point","text":"TreeSet: Backed by a NavigableMap (specifically a TreeMap), ensuring elements are sorted according to their natural ordering or a custom Comparator."},{"order":15,"type":"subheading","text":"4. Map Internals: HashMap"},{"order":16,"type":"paragraph","text":"HashMap stores key-value pairs in 'buckets'. It calculates the hash code of the key to determine which bucket the entry belongs to."},{"order":17,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Hash Collision: When two different keys produce the same hash code, Java handles collisions by storing multiple entries in the same bucket using a linked list. Since Java 8, if a bucket exceeds a threshold (TREEIFY_THRESHOLD = 8), the linked list transforms into a balanced Red-Black tree to improve worst-case performance from O(N) to O(log N)."},{"order":18,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Load Factor and Capacity: The default initial capacity is 16, and the default load factor is 0.75. When the number of entries exceeds capacity * load factor, the map undergoes 'rehashing', doubling its capacity."},{"order":19,"type":"code","text":"Map<String, Integer> map = new HashMap<>();
map.put("Java", 11);
map.put("Python", 3);
int val = map.get("Java");"},{"order":20,"type":"subheading","text":"5. Map Internals: TreeMap"},{"order":21,"type":"paragraph","text":"TreeMap is a Red-Black tree based NavigableMap implementation. Unlike HashMap, which offers O(1) performance and no ordering guarantees, TreeMap guarantees O(log N) performance forcontainsKey, get, put, and remove operations while keeping keys sorted."},{"order":22,"type":"code","text":"Map<String, String> treeMap = new TreeMap<>();
treeMap.put("Zebra", "Stripes");
treeMap.put("Apple", "Fruit");
// Keys will be automatically sorted alphabetically: Apple, Zebra"},{"order":23,"type":"subheading","text":"6. Conclusion and Best Practices"},{"order":24,"type":"paragraph","text":"Choosing the right collection depends heavily on your performance and ordering requirements. Use ArrayList for general lists, HashSet for fast uniqueness checks, HashMap for fast key-value lookups, and TreeMap or TreeSet when sorted order is strictly required."}]
